{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on request bdcd5111, identifiers (c+d), on contract V2 for the\noriginal birth bytes beside a source read. Four decisions.\n\n1. The allowance: cap A. One response spends one `maxBytes` across the\n   body, the birth and the labels that it returns, and `maxBytes` is at\n   most the caller's `entryBytes`. No new allowance and no new ledger.\n   The cutover retires every recorded room, so no stored entry loses a\n   reader. The new format has this limit: a scope reads another\n   scope's later entry only when that entry and the first entry of its\n   sequence fit one entry allowance together. State it in the\n   implementation note and in docs/limits or the page that holds the\n   entry bound. Give the over-allowance stop its own reason word in the\n   reader's unavailable answer, so an author sees the cause. Report in\n   the note the genesis sizes of the platform scopes in the corpus,\n   as measured, so the headroom is a number. If a bound at sealing is\n   cheap (a later entry plus its own genesis within `entryBytes`),\n   propose it. Do not build it under this ruling.\n2. The two-row origin, own genesis and a later creator. The kind of\n   the creator needs no creator birth. Ruling b02334db puts\n   `sourceKind` in the own genesis, and `judgeGenesis` requires that\n   the seed's creator is the source. So the reader takes the creator's\n   kind from the own genesis row, which the response holds. Where an\n   existing nested read already returns the creator's original birth,\n   keep those bytes through the merge, as you propose. Add no row, no\n   field and no read. A branch that needs the creator's genesis for\n   more than the kind, and holds none, gives its existing incomplete or\n   unavailable answer. Tell me which branches those are after the\n   change, with the witness of each. I expect few or none.\n3. The owner and the deadline. Approved as the contract recommends.\n   The read group of `fetchFacts` is the owner. It takes one monotonic\n   deadline and its current identity before the group starts, passes\n   `{maxBytes, milliseconds}` on the one existing `Peer.source` call,\n   and refuses a result that arrives late or for a stale owner, before\n   a Foreign value is usable. No timer, map, queue, retry, cache, RPC\n   or SQL is added. The note says that a logical timeout does not\n   cancel a physical call.\n4. The validation work. No separate counter. Under cap A the work of\n   a group is bounded by the bytes that it admits: at most 128 reads\n   of `maxBytes` each, 33,554,432 bytes at the defaults. The note\n   states that bound as the work bound of this legacy path. The\n   Native debit callback of the partition path is unchanged.\n\nThe nine-row incident origin keeps its exact map, with original bytes\nkept through the merge. The receiver matches the full returned `at`\nagainst the requested SequenceRef before it uses any byte.\n\nThe question on verified own and retained foreign copies is separate.\nAsk it when its contract is ready.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}